# Operand evaluation order with a side-effecting function.
#
# What the standard says: ed.3 7.3.1 (expressions) defines operator
# precedence and says operators of equal precedence are applied left to
# right, but it does not say in which order the OPERANDS of a binary
# operator are evaluated. Moreover functions "shall not contain internal
# state" and a function whose VAR_IN_OUT argument is modified is a side
# effect the standard does not reason about; passing the same variable to
# two calls in one expression is not forbidden but its outcome is not
# specified. So y may be -19 (left operand first), -8 (right operand first)
# or anything else.
#
# What IronPLC 0.251.0 (5ea0610) does: left operand first, then the right
# operand, each BUMP writing back to c immediately:
#   y = 1 - 10 * 2 = -19 with c = 2 after the cycle;
#   without reset, c keeps growing: y = 3 - 10 * 4 = -37, c = 4.
# Recorded 2026-10-31.
period_ms: 100
program: main
inputs: reset
outputs: y c
status: implementation-defined
reference: IEC 61131-3 ed.3 7.3.1 (expressions), 6.6.1.1 (functions)
---
0 reset=TRUE => y=-19 c=2
1 reset=TRUE => y=-19 c=2
2 reset=FALSE => y=-37 c=4
